---
/**
 * Lo-fi scratch page for working out the Why section's exposition and diagrams before building the
 * real thing on the landing page. Not linked from anywhere; delete once Why is settled.
 */
import TreeDiagram from '../../components/scratch/TreeDiagram.astro';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <title>Why scratch</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <p class="tag">scratch · why</p>
      <h1>Config and mocking are a pain. We can do better.</h1>

      <section>
        <h2>1. Your app is a tree</h2>
        <p>
          Components render components. Down at the leaves, the real work happens: fetch the orders,
          check who's signed in, log an error. That work lives in services, and services need other
          services. Every API client needs the HTTP client, and the HTTP client needs config.
        </p>
        <TreeDiagram uses="import" />
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>2. Imports set it in stone</h2>
        <p>
          Usually each piece imports what it needs. <code>OrdersTable</code> imports the orders API,
          which imports the HTTP client, which imports config. That's simple until you want a
          different one: a fake API in a test, a staging config in a preview build.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then you're mocking modules by file path. The mock covers the whole test file, and it only
          catches imports that resolve to the same path you mocked.
        </p>
        <TreeDiagram
          uses="import"
          hot={['OrdersTable>OrdersApi', 'OrdersApi>HttpClient', 'HttpClient>Config']}
          marks={{ OrdersTable: 'hot' }}
          notes={{ HttpClient: "vi.mock('./http')", Config: 'fixed at load time' }}
        />
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>3. Pass it in instead</h2>
        <p>
          Dependency injection is just arguments: <code>OrdersTable</code> takes an orders API
          instead of importing one, and a test passes in a fake. No mocking.
        </p>
        <p>
          But someone has to build the real ones. That's the <em>composition root</em>, usually your
          entry point. It builds every service in the right order, then hands them down through
          every layer. <code>Header</code> and <code>Dashboard</code> never use the auth client or
          the orders API, but they have to take them and pass them on. Give a leaf a new service and
          you edit every component on the way down.
        </p>
        <TreeDiagram
          uses="prop"
          marks={{ App: 'root', Header: 'pass', Dashboard: 'pass' }}
          edgeLabels={{
            'App>Header': 'auth',
            'App>Dashboard': 'orders, analytics',
            'Header>UserMenu': 'auth',
            'Dashboard>OrdersTable': 'orders',
            'Dashboard>RevenueChart': 'analytics',
          }}
        />
        <pre><code>{`// main.tsx: build everything, in order, up front
const config = loadConfig();
const http = createHttpClient(config);
const logger = createLogger(config);
const auth = createAuthClient(http);
const orders = createOrdersApi(http);
const analytics = createAnalyticsApi(http, logger);

render(<App auth={auth} orders={orders} analytics={analytics} />);

// App.tsx: pass it all down
function App({ auth, orders, analytics }) {
  return (
    <>
      <Header auth={auth} />
      <Dashboard orders={orders} analytics={analytics} />
    </>
  );
}`}</code></pre>
        <p class="aside">
          If a child needs its own instance, or the root shouldn't build things nobody uses, you
          pass factories instead of instances. Same plumbing, more of it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>4. Ask for it where you need it</h2>
        <p>
          With Hypospray, each piece asks for what it needs with <code>inject()</code>.{' '}
          <code>OrdersTable</code> asks for the orders API, and the injector builds it, building the
          HTTP client and config first. Each is built once, when something first asks, and shared
          after that. <code>Header</code> and <code>Dashboard</code> go back to knowing nothing
          about services.
        </p>
        <TreeDiagram uses="inject()" />
        <pre><code>{`// orders-api.ts
export function createOrdersApi(http = inject(createHttpClient)) {
  // ...
}

// OrdersTable.tsx
function OrdersTable() {
  const orders = inject(createOrdersApi);
  // ...
}`}</code></pre>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>5. Swap anything, from the top</h2>
        <p>
          Want every request to hit a fake server? Give the injector a fake HTTP client. Everything
          that asks for one gets the fake, however deep it is. Nothing in between changes.
        </p>
        <p>
          And because <code>inject()</code> is only a default, you can always pass an argument
          yourself and skip Hypospray entirely.
        </p>
        <TreeDiagram
          uses="inject()"
          marks={{ HttpClient: 'fake' }}
          hot={['AuthClient>HttpClient', 'OrdersApi>HttpClient', 'AnalyticsApi>HttpClient']}
          notes={{ HttpClient: 'fake' }}
        />
        <pre><code>{`const injector = createInjector({
  providers: [provideValue(createHttpClient, fakeHttp)],
});`}</code></pre>
      </section>

      <section class="questions">
        <h2>Open questions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Beat 4 shows <code>inject()</code> inside a component. Core has no framework binding, so
            today that needs <code>injector.run()</code> around rendering, or a binding we haven't
            written. Is that a promise the page can make?
          </li>
          <li>
            Should beat 3 mention React context (and friends)? It fixes the drilling for components,
            but it's tied to the framework and doesn't help services get their dependencies.
          </li>
          <li>
            Show laziness (a route nobody visits never builds its services), or leave it to docs?
          </li>
          <li>Sticky graph vs. one diagram per beat: decide once the story works.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  body {
    margin: 0;
    background: #fff;
    color: #222;
    font:
      17px/1.5 system-ui,
      sans-serif;
  }
  main {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px 16px 96px;
  }
  .tag {
    color: #999;
    font-size: 13px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: 28px;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  section {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 20px;
  }
  code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    background: #f6f6f6;
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .aside {
    color: #666;
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .questions {
    padding: 16px 20px;
    background: #fffbe6;
  }
  .questions h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
